(Ord. No. 1036, § 1(Exh. A), 2-15-2017)

579:
(a) The Mayor and City Council finds that signs provide an important medium through which persons may convey a variety of messages. However, left completely unregulated, signs can become a threat to public safety as a traffic hazard and a detriment to property values and to the City's general public welfare, as well as create an aesthetic nuisance. By enacting this Chapter, the Mayor and City Council intends to:
(1) Balance the rights of persons to convey their messages through signs and the right of the public to be protected against the unrestricted proliferation of signs;
(2) Protect the public health, safety, and welfare of the citizens and others within the City;
(3) Reduce traffic hazards, pedestrian hazards, and other hazards;
(4) Promote and maintain the aesthetic qualities of the City;
(5) Protect property values by minimizing the possible adverse effects and visual blight caused by signs;
(6) Promote economic development;
(7) Ensure the fair and consistent enforcement of sign regulations;
(8) Promote the stated purposes of the City Zoning Ordinance, as amended, which are expressly incorporated herein;
(9) Promote the stated purposes of the International Building Code, as adopted and modified by the City, which are expressly incorporated herein; and
(10) Except to the extent allowed by applicable federal and state law, nothing herein is intended to in any way preempt any federal or state law requirements regarding signs or to exempt any person or entity from compliance therewith.
(b) Some signage has a single targeted function and identification of such signage by description is impossible without referring to its function. For instance, address numerals are used for the sole purpose of providing addresses, which is of benefit to persons looking for those addresses and is essential to public safety personnel responding to emergencies. Signs at the entrances to or within subdivisions or major developments favor a similar purpose in enabling both the traveling public and emergency personnel to quickly locate entrances and internal sites for the purpose of either visitation or responding to emergency calls. While such signage is referenced based upon the function it serves within the context of this section, the provisions of this section are unrelated to the content of the speech provided and allow maximum expressive potential to sign owners.
(Ord. No. 1302, § 1(Exh. A), 2-15-2023)
